# A KNOWN LIMITATION, PINNED AS CURRENT BEHAVIOUR. This file passes today and MUST go red
# the moment the move checker becomes path-sensitive. When it does, that is the fix landing,
# not a regression: delete this file.
#
# MM.md finding F4: the move checker is not path-sensitive. Returning the same owning value
# from two MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE branches is reported as a use-after-move, because the checker
# marks `val` moved at the first return and then sees the second return as a use of a moved
# value. Only one of them can ever run.
#
# The limitation is old, but it was invisible until Phase 9: before the string tier flipped,
# a `string` was a COPY type, so this shape never moved anything and never reported. Making a
# string move is what exposed it. It applies equally to any owning type -- a `T[]`, a `List`,
# an `Own` -- and always did.
#
# The workaround is to return from one place, which is what
# tests/generics/test_run_issue214_non_name_arg_inference.sushi now does. That file exists to
# pin issue #214 and merely tripped over this; restructuring it there and pinning the
# limitation here keeps both facts asserted instead of trading one for the other.
# EXPECT_ERROR_CODE: CE2405

fn pick(i32 which, string val) string:
    if (which > 0):
        return Result.Ok(val)
    return Result.Ok(val)

fn run() i32:
    let i32 n = 1
    let string s = "hi{n}"
    let string got = pick(1, s)??
    println(got)
    return Result.Ok(0)

fn main() i32:
    let i32 rc = run().realise(1)
    return Result.Ok(rc)
